Access Control & IDOR
Access control is one of the most critical security mechanisms in any application. It determines who is allowed to access which resources and perform which actions. When implemented incorrectly, it can lead to severe vulnerabilities that allow attackers to access or modify data belonging to other users.
This document explains common access control failures including IDOR, horizontal privilege escalation, vertical privilege escalation, mass assignment, and missing function-level access control.


1. Access Control Fundamentals
Access control ensures that authenticated users can only perform actions and access data they are permitted to.
It works by validating three things:

User Identity
      |
      v
Authentication
(Who are you?)
      |
      v
Authorization
(What are you allowed to do?)
      |
      v
Resource Access
Many applications stop at authentication, assuming that once a user is logged in, they can access certain resources. This assumption often leads to authorization failures.


Types of Access Control
There are several models used in application security.
Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)
Permissions are assigned based on roles.
Example:

User Role → Permissions
-----------------------
Admin → Manage users, modify system
Manager → View reports
Customer → View own data


Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC)
Access is determined using attributes such as:
	• user attributes
	• resource attributes
	• environmental conditions
Example:

Allow access if:
User.department = Finance
AND
Resource.type = FinancialReport


Object-Level Access Control
Each resource belongs to a specific user.
Example:

Order ID 1001 → belongs to User A
Order ID 1002 → belongs to User B
The application must verify ownership before returning the resource.


2. Access Control Failures
Access control vulnerabilities occur when applications fail to enforce authorization checks properly.
Common failures include:

Access Control Failures
|
|-- IDOR (Broken Object Level Authorization)
|-- Horizontal Privilege Escalation
|-- Vertical Privilege Escalation
|-- Mass Assignment
|-- Missing Function-Level Access Control
These vulnerabilities allow attackers to:
	• access other users' data
	• perform unauthorized actions
	• escalate privileges


3. IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference)
Definition
IDOR occurs when an application exposes a reference to an internal object (such as a database ID) and fails to verify whether the requesting user is authorized to access it.
Attackers exploit this by modifying identifiers in requests.


How IDOR Happens
Applications often use identifiers such as:
	• user IDs
	• order IDs
	• invoice numbers
	• file names
Example request:

GET /api/orders/1001
If the backend only retrieves the record but does not verify ownership, attackers can modify the identifier.

GET /api/orders/1002
If order 1002 belongs to another user, the attacker gains unauthorized access.


IDOR Attack Flow

Attacker logged in as User A
        |
        v
Intercept request
GET /api/orders/1001
        |
        v
Modify object ID
GET /api/orders/1002
        |
        v
Server retrieves order 1002
        |
        X No ownership validation
        |
        v
Attacker receives another user's data


Example Vulnerable Backend Code
Example of a vulnerable implementation:

@app.route("/order/<order_id>")
def get_order(order_id):
    order = db.get_order(order_id)
    return order
The application retrieves the order directly using the ID without verifying ownership.


Secure Implementation
The application must validate ownership before returning the object.

@app.route("/order/<order_id>")
def get_order(order_id):
    order = db.get_order(order_id)

    if order.user_id != current_user.id:
        return "Unauthorized", 403

    return order
Now the request will only succeed if the order belongs to the authenticated user.


Why Developers Accidentally Introduce IDOR
IDOR usually appears because developers assume that:
	• authentication automatically implies authorization
	• IDs are unpredictable
	• front-end controls are sufficient
However, attackers can easily manipulate requests using tools such as proxies.


Detection Techniques
Security teams detect IDOR through:
Manual Testing
Modify identifiers in requests.
Example:

/api/account/4321
/api/account/4322
/api/account/4323
If responses return different users' data, the application is vulnerable.


Automated Security Testing
Security tools may detect patterns where object identifiers appear in requests without authorization checks.
However, manual testing is usually more effective.


Secure Architecture Principles
To prevent IDOR vulnerabilities:
Enforce authorization at the backend
Never rely on client-side checks.


Validate object ownership
Every resource request must verify ownership.

User → Request resource
        |
        v
Backend retrieves object
        |
        v
Validate ownership
        |
      Yes → return resource
      No  → deny request


Use indirect references
Instead of exposing database IDs, applications can use random identifiers.
Example:

/orders/7f4a9e21-8bcd-43f7
However, this should not replace authorization checks.


Centralize authorization logic
Authorization logic should be implemented in middleware or service layers, rather than repeated across controllers.

Privilege Escalation:
Privilege escalation vulnerabilities occur when a user gains permissions beyond what they were originally granted.
These vulnerabilities are extremely dangerous because they allow attackers to move from normal user → privileged user.
There are two primary types:

Privilege Escalation
│
├── Horizontal Privilege Escalation
│
└── Vertical Privilege Escalation
Understanding the difference between these two is critical for security engineers and threat modelers.

4. Horizontal Privilege Escalation
Definition
Horizontal privilege escalation occurs when a user gains access to resources belonging to another user with the same privilege level.
In simple terms:

User A → Accesses data of → User B
Both users have the same role, but the application fails to enforce resource ownership checks.

Example Scenario
Consider an application that allows users to view their profile information.

GET /api/users/123/profile
If the server only verifies that the user is authenticated, but does not verify that the user owns the profile, an attacker can change the identifier.

GET /api/users/124/profile
If profile 124 belongs to another user, the attacker can access that information.

Attack Flow

User A logs into application
        │
        ▼
User requests profile
GET /api/users/123/profile
        │
        ▼
Attacker intercepts request
        │
        ▼
Modify identifier
GET /api/users/124/profile
        │
        ▼
Server returns User B's data
The server never validated ownership, which resulted in unauthorized access.

Real World Impact
Horizontal privilege escalation can expose sensitive user data such as:
	• Personal information
	• Financial records
	• Order histories
	• Private messages
	• Medical records
In large-scale systems, this can lead to mass data exposure.

Vulnerable Implementation Example

@app.route("/profile/<user_id>")
def get_profile(user_id):
    profile = database.get_user(user_id)
    return profile
The backend retrieves the requested profile directly.
No check ensures that the logged-in user matches the requested profile.

Secure Implementation

@app.route("/profile/<user_id>")
def get_profile(user_id):

    if user_id != current_user.id:
        return "Unauthorized", 403

    profile = database.get_user(user_id)
    return profile
Now the application ensures that users can only access their own profile.

Prevention Strategies
Always Validate Resource Ownership
Every request must confirm that the user owns the requested object.

User → Request resource
        │
        ▼
Retrieve resource
        │
        ▼
Check ownership
        │
   ┌────┴────┐
   │         │
Allowed   Denied

Avoid Direct Object References
Instead of exposing sequential identifiers like:

/api/orders/1001
/api/orders/1002
/api/orders/1003
Use unpredictable identifiers:

/api/orders/a82bd-83jd-39dj
However, this does not replace authorization checks.

5. Vertical Privilege Escalation
Definition
Vertical privilege escalation occurs when a user gains access to functionality reserved for higher privilege roles.
Example:

User → becomes → Admin
Unlike horizontal escalation, this vulnerability allows attackers to perform administrative actions.

Example Scenario
Consider an admin panel endpoint:

POST /admin/deleteUser
If the application only checks whether the user is logged in, any authenticated user may be able to call this endpoint.

Attack Flow

Normal User Account
        │
        ▼
Intercept request to admin API
        │
        ▼
Call privileged endpoint

POST /admin/deleteUser
        │
        ▼
Server executes admin action
Because role verification is missing, the action succeeds.

Real World Consequences
Vertical privilege escalation can allow attackers to:
	• Delete users
	• Modify permissions
	• Access internal dashboards
	• Extract sensitive system data
	• Control the application
In worst cases, attackers gain full administrative control.

Vulnerable Backend Example

@app.route("/admin/deleteUser", methods=["POST"])
def delete_user():

    user_id = request.json["user_id"]
    database.delete_user(user_id)

    return "User deleted"
This endpoint performs a critical action but does not verify the caller's role.

Secure Implementation

@app.route("/admin/deleteUser", methods=["POST"])
def delete_user():

    if current_user.role != "admin":
        return "Forbidden", 403

    user_id = request.json["user_id"]
    database.delete_user(user_id)

    return "User deleted"
The system now enforces role-based authorization.

Secure Architecture Pattern
Authorization should ideally be enforced before reaching business logic.

Client Request
      │
      ▼
Authentication Middleware
      │
      ▼
Authorization Middleware
      │
      ▼
Business Logic
This approach ensures that unauthorized requests never reach sensitive operations.

Key Difference Between Horizontal and Vertical Escalation

Horizontal Escalation
User A → accesses → User B data

Vertical Escalation
User → gains → higher privilege role
Both vulnerabilities stem from improper authorization checks.

Mass Assignment & Missing Function Level Access Control
Access control vulnerabilities are not always about reading someone else's data.
Sometimes the problem lies in how applications process user input and expose sensitive functionality.
Two common examples are:

Access Control Failures
│
├── Mass Assignment
│
└── Missing Function Level Access Control
Both vulnerabilities occur when applications trust user input or API access too much.


6. Mass Assignment
Definition
Mass Assignment occurs when an application automatically binds user input to internal object properties without proper filtering.
In simple terms:

User Input → Automatically mapped → Application Object
If sensitive fields are not protected, attackers can modify properties they should never control.


How Mass Assignment Happens
Modern frameworks automatically convert request parameters into application objects.
Example:

POST /api/updateUser
Request body:

{
  "username": "alice",
  "email": "alice@example.com"
}
The backend maps these fields directly into a user object.

Request JSON → User Object
But if the attacker sends additional hidden parameters:

{
  "username": "alice",
  "email": "alice@example.com",
  "role": "admin"
}
And the backend blindly binds the fields, the attacker can elevate their privileges.


Attack Flow

Attacker registers normal account
        │
        ▼
Attacker inspects API request
        │
        ▼
Finds update endpoint

POST /api/updateUser
        │
        ▼
Attacker adds hidden parameter

role=admin
        │
        ▼
Server automatically binds fields
        │
        ▼
User becomes admin
The vulnerability occurs because the application trusts client-controlled input.


Real World Impact
Mass assignment vulnerabilities have historically led to:
	• privilege escalation
	• account takeover
	• unauthorized feature access
	• security control bypass
Some real incidents occurred in frameworks like Ruby on Rails, where automatic object binding exposed sensitive properties.


Vulnerable Backend Example

def update_user(request):

    user = User(**request.json)
    database.save(user)

    return "Profile updated"
Here the backend blindly assigns all request parameters to the User object.
If the attacker adds hidden fields like:

isAdmin=true
accountStatus=active
role=superuser
they may change internal application state.


Secure Implementation
Instead of accepting all fields, applications should explicitly define allowed parameters.

def update_user(request):

    allowed_fields = {
        "username": request.json["username"],
        "email": request.json["email"]
    }

    user.update(allowed_fields)
    database.save(user)

    return "Profile updated"
Now only safe fields are processed.


Secure Design Pattern
The safest approach is to implement allow-list input mapping.

Incoming Request
      │
      ▼
Filter Allowed Fields
      │
      ▼
Bind Safe Fields
      │
      ▼
Update Object
Anything not explicitly allowed is ignored.


Additional Protection Strategies
Use DTOs (Data Transfer Objects)
Instead of binding request data directly to domain objects:

Request → DTO → Validation → Domain Object
This prevents accidental modification of internal properties.


Protect Sensitive Fields
Fields such as the following must never be client-controlled:

role
isAdmin
accountStatus
permissions
accountBalance
These fields should only be modified by trusted server-side logic.


7. Missing Function-Level Access Control
Definition
Missing Function-Level Access Control occurs when an application fails to verify whether a user is authorized to execute a specific function.
This usually affects administrative APIs or sensitive backend operations.


Example Scenario
Consider an administrative endpoint:

GET /admin/exportUsers
This endpoint exports the entire user database.
If the server only checks authentication:

if user.isAuthenticated():
then any logged-in user may call this endpoint.


Attack Flow

Attacker logs into normal user account
        │
        ▼
Attacker discovers admin API endpoint

/admin/exportUsers
        │
        ▼
Attacker directly calls endpoint
        │
        ▼
Server executes request
        │
        ▼
Sensitive data exposed
The application assumed that the frontend would hide the endpoint, but attackers interact directly with APIs.


Why This Happens
Developers sometimes rely on UI restrictions instead of backend enforcement.
Example:

Admin button hidden in UI
But the backend API still exists:

POST /admin/deleteUser
Attackers simply send the request manually.


Vulnerable Backend Example

@app.route("/admin/exportUsers")
def export_users():

    data = database.get_all_users()
    return data
The function performs an administrative action but does not verify user role.


Secure Implementation
Every sensitive function must verify user privileges.

@app.route("/admin/exportUsers")
def export_users():

    if current_user.role != "admin":
        return "Forbidden", 403

    data = database.get_all_users()
    return data
This ensures that only authorized users can access the function.


Secure Architecture Pattern
Function-level authorization should be enforced using centralized middleware.

Client Request
      │
      ▼
Authentication Layer
      │
      ▼
Authorization Layer
      │
      ▼
Role / Permission Check
      │
      ▼
Application Function
Centralizing authorization prevents developers from accidentally skipping checks.


Common Mistake Developers Make
Many applications enforce authorization only at the UI level.

UI → Hides admin features
API → Still accessible
Attackers bypass the UI completely.

Attacker → Direct API Request → Sensitive Function


Key Security Lessons

Mass Assignment
User modifies internal object properties

Missing Function-Level Access Control
User executes privileged backend functions
Both vulnerabilities occur because applications trust the client too much.
Security must always be enforced server-side.


